t being told very fundamental facts  that would influence your understanding  and opinion of the debt ceiling I'm  gonna tell you three important things  about the debt ceiling  number one the debt ceiling does not  actually limit limit government deficits  why because spending is  arised by separate legislation you can  pass any debt ceiling limit that you  want and it will have no impact on  Congress passing any spending that they  want in separate bills or legislation it  will prevent the Treasury from paying  for those expenditures and that's where  you start hearing about government  shutdown but we are talking about two  totally different things  number two the debt ceiling is  completely separate from the federal  budget no matter what the debt ceiling  is a budget can be passed that increases  the deficit and thus the debt decreases  it whatever the debt ceiling has no  impact on the budget deficit that might  be passed or added or whatever the case  may be and number three and this is  maybe the most important thing people  often don't get when they just say hey  we shouldn't be increasing the debt  ceiling constantly the debt ceiling is  an aggregate figure okay that means it's  a number of dollars this is probably the  most relevant and most often missing  fact lots of politicians complain we  can't just constantly raise the debt  ceiling well we have a country whose  economy grows and there's inflation  here's how to think about this think  about a small business right imagine you  have a small business the revenues are a  hundred thousand dollars per year you  say okay let's set our debt limit the  most we're willing to borrow how far  into debt were willing to go to remain  in good financial shape we'll call it  five thousand dollars okay that's our  debt ceiling it works out to five  percent you've got a hundred thousand  dollar business think of it as the  economy and you set the debt ceiling at  five thousand dollars next year business  grows to two hundred thousand dollars  and in order to support that growth and  accelerated we say oh we want to buy  more machines we want to hire more  people we want to use debt in order to  do that but damn our debt ceiling is  five thousand dollars but because the  business has grown that same five  thousand dollar debt limit now only  represents two and a half percent of the  business so really if you're comfortable  carrying five percent of your business  in debt you should raise the debt  ceiling to ten thousand dollars from  five thousand dollars  oh we doubled our debt ceiling but it's  still just 5% of your business this is  what people miss the American economy  grows and we also have inflation if you  set a debt ceiling in dollars rather  than in percent as your country grows  and as prices rise due to inflation you  have to raise the debt ceiling  constantly without it necessarily  representing an increase in the portion  of your economy that the debt represents  now this is a separate argument from  what should the debt ceiling be but any  complaint from a politician that they  are against regularly raising the debt  ceiling well in context compared to what  I hope everybody understands what I'm  saying here right debt ceiling is a  billion and then Congress passes and  says well it'll be 2 billion well how  does that relate to the size of the  economy all right it's that sort of  clear pat like is that does that make  sense in general it's a whole thing it's  a whole rundown but as you'd imagine the  size of the economy grows you have to  raise it by more and more in terms of  pure dollars raw dollar that's the idea  that's the general idea so let's cut to  Trump right Trump cuts a deal with  Democratic leaders Nancy Pelosi in the  house Chuck Schumer in the Senate to  raise the debt limit short term in  connection with also needing hurricane  relief funds this would be a three month  raised to the debt ceiling this I  actually believe is an unforced error  for 